# TODO - Adaptive accelerator resource budgeting

## Objective

Discover available memory and compute resources at runtime and choose batch
size, state layout, caches, and search breadth accordingly. Tiny devices around
Small-memory devices must remain usable while additional addressable
resources on arbitrarily larger devices must increase available work rather than
hitting a fixed artificial VRAM ceiling. The 128 MiB and 80 GiB values are only
example probe points, not supported-range endpoints.

## Acceptance

- The scheduler runs within measured memory limits from approximately 128 MiB
  through large-memory accelerators and converts additional resources into
  measured throughput/search breadth without fixed-size assumptions.
- A CPU/reference path remains sufficient for correctness, and accelerator
  failure/unavailability changes performance rather than semantic acceptance.
- The work states a falsifiable question or hypothesis, an explicit baseline,
  and an observation that would reject or materially weaken the proposed
  technique before performance conclusions are accepted.
- If executable algorithm research is required, the stable ID is mirrored under
  `docs/research/algorithms/<id>/` and `algorithms/<id>/`; ordinary product
  engineering is not forced into that mirror.
- Performance conclusions use equivalent workloads and report raw-sample
  provenance, resource budgets, dispersion/uncertainty, and failure/success
  behavior rather than only a best-case number.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `accelerator/`, `algorithms/`,
  `optimizer/`, `benchmarks/accelerator/`, `tests/optimizer/`.
- Required evidence: CPU/reference differential results, device/resource
  metadata, failure/fallback tests, and benchmark samples for claimed speedups.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `cuda-exact-vm-adapter`,
  `reproducible-experiment-identity-and-manifest`.
- Active correctness slice: `accelerator/resource_budget.py` validates measured
  resources and greedily partitions exact resident item bytes below a deterministic
  reserve. Classic CUDA reads memory through `cuMemGetInfo_v2`, reads compute
  capacity through `cuDeviceGetAttribute`, and uses the resulting plan before
  allocation. Oversized single items fail before `cuMemAlloc`.
- Synthetic boundary evidence is explicitly labeled: the declared current-state
  byte model admits 6 first-chunk states at 128 MiB and 4,209 at 80 GiB. These
  are example probes rather than minimum/maximum supported sizes. A separate
  100,000 GiB synthetic fixture proves that additional addressable memory is not
  clamped; classic CUDA splits only at its 32-bit memory-index boundary. None of
  these synthetic rows are hardware throughput measurements.
- Retained post-commit resource evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-resource-budget-windows-x86_64/`
  records the RTX 4060 Driver API snapshot and synthetic boundary rows with exact
  commit/toolchain/device provenance. It is capacity evidence only.
- Post-commit no-ceiling evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-unbounded-vram-windows-x86_64/`
  retains the 100,000 GiB / 100,000-item synthetic case. All requested work is
  admitted and split only at the classic backend's 32-bit launch-index boundary.
- Current-profile resident execution now consumes the same measured resource
  planner with exact 4,782,969-word item sizing; full-state RTX 4060 differential
  evidence passes. Retained RTX 4060 throughput/phase samples now cover the
  current profile; broader live-hardware evidence remains open.
- Retained end-to-end throughput evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-classic-throughput-rtx4060/`
  contains 15 raw samples for batches 1/8/32/128 on an exact 64-step workload.
  Median throughput falls from 29.127 VMs/s at batch 1 to 27.751 VMs/s at batch
  128 (~4.73% slower), rejecting a current claim that larger batches alone
  improve throughput. Phase-separated timing is required before selecting an
  optimization.
- Phase attribution is retained under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-classic-phase-profile-rtx4060/`.
  At batch 128, kernel+sync is about 0.0097% of total wall time while
  validation/planning + host construction + decode account for about 97.75%.
  This selects host representation/validation as the next measured bottleneck.
- First post-optimization comparison is retained under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-classic-phase-profile-array-rtx4060/`.
  Batch-8 median wall time falls 4.35x and batch-128 falls 4.04x versus the
  phase-profile baseline after contiguous host buffers and duplicate-validation
  removal. Validation/planning becomes the dominant measured phase.
- Aggregate-validation evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-classic-phase-profile-minmax-rtx4060/`
  reduces batch-128 median wall time to 636,002,300 ns (7.39x faster than the
  original phase baseline) and reaches about 201.26 VMs/s. Host construction is
  again the largest phase.
- Shared-memory batch evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-classic-phase-profile-shared-memory-rtx4060/`
  reaches about 1,076.38 VMs/s at batch 128 and lowers wall time about 39.5x versus
  the original phase baseline. Complete Python result materialization is now the
  dominant phase (~75%).
- The original current-profile baseline under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-current-profile-throughput-rtx4060/`
  selected host-state movement as the next optimization boundary. Post-change
  evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-27-current-profile-resident-session-rtx4060/`
  raises complete-snapshot batch 32 from about 40.08 to 51.67 VMs/s and cuts
  median upload time about 6.93x through device-side shared initialization. A
  persistent scalable session reaches about 2.00 million 64-step VM segments/s
  at batch 128 when setup/observation/snapshots are excluded from `advance()`.
  `ProfileMemoryImage` now validates/owns reusable geometry-bound inputs once;
  retained batch-32 validation/planning falls from about 62.40 ms to 0.23 ms
  and complete throughput reaches about 93.68 VMs/s. Direct snapshot output now
  removes redundant packed host staging. `ProfileSnapshotPhaseProfile` and
  `profile_resident_snapshot_phase_profile.py` add a diagnostic-only decomposition
  of fresh host arrays, scalar-state/full-memory/output D-to-H, decode, and total.
  Retained evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-28-current-profile-resident-snapshot-phase-profile-rtx4060/`
  records 3.1616/65.7829/271.1391 ms for batches 1/8/32. Batch 1 is 96.489%
  memory transfer; batches 8/32 are 62.419%/63.872% allocation and
  37.248%/35.962% transfer. Decode is at most 0.105% and named coverage is at
  least 99.817%. Ordinary snapshot semantics remain unchanged. Independent mutable result ownership is now split explicitly. Ordinary
  snapshots allocate fresh arrays at every batch size; the active
  `caller-owned-independent-u32-arrays-v1` workspace allocates once and documents
  overwrite/alias semantics. Retained evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-28-current-profile-snapshot-workspace-tradeoff-rtx4060/`
  records ordinary/workspace batches 1/8/32 at 8.1785/3.1631,
  65.7327/24.6510, and 272.1251/100.3275 ms (2.586x/2.667x/2.712x).
  One-time allocation is 4.9179/41.3042/173.7859 ms and crossover is 1/2/2;
  batch-one margin is explicitly marginal. Workspace buffers retain
  18.246/145.965/583.859 MiB and their hot path is at least 96.485% memory D-to-H.
  Retained evidence under `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-28-current-profile-snapshot-host-registration-tradeoff-rtx4060/` promotes
  `bounded-all-or-pageable-u32-arrays-v1` for explicit workspaces. A 256 MiB
  all-or-none budget registers batches 1/8, improving 1.079x/1.108x with crossover
  2/3, and forces batch 32 to `budget-exceeded` pageable fallback. Driver rejection
  rolls back cleanly; all page locks release with workspace/session/runtime lifetime;
  ordinary snapshots and default workspaces remain pageable. Retained streaming
  evidence under `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-29-current-profile-snapshot-stream-window-tradeoff-rtx4060/` promotes
  `caller-owned-windowed-u32-arrays-v1`. Batch-32 windows 1/8 reduce retained host
  memory 96.875%/75.000% and improve 1.023x/1.036x versus the full pageable window.
  Window 1 minimizes memory; window 8 is the measured balanced point on this device.
  Ordered CUDA D-to-H lifetime is explicit through
  `cuda-ordered-registered-dtoh-stream-v1`. The admitted
  `caller-owned-double-window-overlap-u32-arrays-v1` workspace uses two equal banks
  only when the total host budget and all registrations succeed; every other case
  follows exact synchronous fallback. Retained evidence under
  `benchmarks/accelerator/evidence/2026-07-29-current-profile-snapshot-double-buffer-overlap-rtx4060/` measures
  1.003x/1.012x for matched windows 1/8 with 14/15 and 15/15 paired wins, at exactly
  twice the retained host memory and higher setup. Adaptive bank/window choice,
  other callback workloads, kernel overlap, and broader live-hardware measurements
  remain required. Persistent classic continuation is tracked by its own retained
  session evidence.
- Active ticket-admission slice: `accelerator/ticket_admission.py` validates
  exact same-context paired route evidence and chooses deterministic queue
  chunks. The retained RTX 4060 full-domain CRAZY profile admits synchronous
  groups 2/4/8, rejects streamed 1/2/4/8, validates the packed workload before
  execution, and leaves the global synchronous default unchanged. Opt-in
  `evidence-bound-ticket-route-admission-report-v1` explains every validated route
  in input order with stable rejection reasons and selected-use counts while
  embedding the unchanged plan. The retained CUDA facade exposes the same report
  only after exact profile resolution. Sixty-one admission/telemetry/persistence tests
  cover report
  identity, fallback, five isolated rejection classes, eligible but unused routes,
  duplicate rejection, exact CUDA equivalence, and the existing planner/executor
  contracts. The schema-v4 product registry is reconstructed byte-for-byte from
  retained evidence, the tracked CUDA toolchain manifest, display-driver build,
  and host/Python context. Twelve manifest tests reject drift, duplicate/unknown
  keys, unsupported schema, duplicate routes, malformed display versions, invalid
  host fields, and duplicate exact runtime contexts while allowing distinct runtime
  variants. Fourteen runtime tests measure Driver API, NVRTC, toolchain SHA, NVML
  display build, Windows host, architecture, and CPython with fake and live routes.
  Missing optional identity leaves ordinary CUDA available but the profile
  unmatched. Runtime and the report never read benchmark evidence, and the report
  performs no online learning. `bounded-ticket-admission-telemetry-v1` retains
  completed reports, while
  `bounded-ticket-admission-failure-telemetry-v1` independently retains stable
  accelerator-failure categories without exception text. Both caller-owned
  positive-capacity FIFOs expose monotonic sequence/eviction state and
  measured-versus-estimated duration. Malformed reports, timings, or foreign
  failures fail before mutation. `TicketAdmissionAttemptTelemetry` pairs the
  FIFOs, and the separate CUDA attempt executor records exactly one outcome
  before returning or re-raising the same accelerator error. Existing ordinary
  and completion-only executors remain unchanged.
  `ticket-admission-telemetry-document-v1` captures both snapshots as compact,
  sorted-key schema-v1 JSON. Decoding defaults to 1 MiB and 4,096 observations
  per FIFO, rejects duplicate, unknown, oversized, and noncanonical input, and
  restores exact sequence/eviction state. Explicit writes use same-directory
  temporary files and atomic replacement. There is no automatic loading, hidden
  worker, automatic promotion, or policy authority. Other hosts, Python versions,
  drivers, devices/workloads, telemetry schema migration or alternate stores,
  and adaptive queue/resource feedback remain open.
